ZAICODE

P0 LONG-HORIZON RELIABILITY / PERFORMANCE + PROVIDER FAILOVER

THIS IS A NEW EXPLICIT USER REQUEST.

Ingest it through the canonical SAIPEN user-request path.

Do not edit the installed SAIPEN protocol implementation as part of this product
work.

The current supplied archive shows an unrelated SAIPEN protocol-maintenance
ticket active in the root workspace. Preserve that work and its evidence.
This request is ZAICODE product work.

SEVERITY

P0:
ZAICODE can degrade after approximately 6-9 hours of continuous operation to
approximately 1-2 FPS and may become effectively unusable.

Observed operator symptoms include:

- severe UI frame-rate collapse after many hours;
- project selection appears to work but the selected project can render an empty
  content area;
- opening projects may stop producing usable content;
- restarting ZAICODE restores normal performance.

This directly violates ZAICODE's primary autonomous-operation purpose.

A program intended to run agents unattended for many hours must not require
periodic application restarts to remain usable.

This request is not satisfied by adding a periodic full application restart.

ROOT CAUSE FIRST.

SECONDARY USER-REPORTED FAILURES

1. When a paid/subscription provider reaches its quota/plan limit, ZAICODE must
   stop repeatedly hitting the exhausted route and automatically fall back to
   SAIFREN according to policy.

2. If the operator changes the selected model/provider after a failure and then
   presses Retry, Retry must use the CURRENT selection.

   Current reported defect:
   GLM reached its limit.
   Operator changed GLM -> SAIFREN.
   Retry still attempted GLM.

3. Antigravity/ZCode rolling quota windows are still not truthful in live use.

   Previous T-143 implemented rolling-window behavior, but the operator still
   observes states such as:
   - "starts at first use";
   - "window started 13 min ago" while no visible active window/countdown exists.

   The operator's intended product behavior is:
   a supported rolling plan window should be actively maintained and visible,
   rather than sitting indefinitely waiting for manual first use.

4. Provider/window state must agree everywhere in the UI.

Do not add unrelated features during this program.

======================================================================
PROGRAM STRUCTURE
======================================================================

Treat this request as one source with three engineering tracks:

TRACK A — P0 LONG-HORIZON RUNTIME STABILITY
TRACK B — QUOTA CIRCUIT BREAKER / SAIFREN FAILOVER
TRACK C — ROLLING WINDOW RUNTIME TRUTH

TRACK A is the priority.

Do not let B/C delay the initial P0 investigation and instrumentation.

======================================================================
TRACK A — P0 LONG-HORIZON RUNTIME STABILITY
======================================================================

A1. REPRODUCE THE ACTUAL LONG-RUN FAILURE

Do not start with speculative optimization.

Use the production-like packaged Electron application.

The existing T-67 performance pass is NOT sufficient acceptance evidence.

T-67 explicitly did not perform the actual desktop long-duration heap/CPU run.
It mainly proved:
- static timer/listener cleanup patterns;
- reduced minute-level clock renders;
- finished audit history capped at 30;
- basic memory diagnostics registered.

The new operator report supersedes the assumption that these checks were enough.

Build a proper long-duration harness.

Required runs:

1. ACCELERATED CHURN RUN
   At least 60-90 minutes with repeated activity designed to expose accumulation.

2. REAL SOAK
   At least 9 continuous hours.
   Prefer 12 hours when execution time allows.

The soak must use the packaged application, not only jsdom/unit tests.

Do not keep the application artificially idle for the entire run.

The harness should perform representative autonomous usage periodically:

- switch projects;
- open sessions;
- start/finish synthetic or safe local tasks;
- append streaming session content;
- open/close Settings;
- open SAIHOME;
- open project folders/slots;
- change active session;
- let quota/timer systems tick;
- allow notifications to arrive/expire;
- exercise SAIMAIL polling without destructive remote actions;
- run background status polling;
- open/close audit/session surfaces;
- leave completed sessions behind;
- revisit older projects.

The workload must be deterministic enough to compare runs.

A2. RECORD RESOURCE TELEMETRY

Capture at regular intervals, preferably every 30-60 seconds:

PROCESS

- Electron main PID;
- renderer PID(s);
- utility/helper processes;
- router process where relevant;
- child agent process count.

MEMORY

- renderer JS heap used;
- renderer JS heap total;
- renderer RSS/private working set;
- Electron main RSS/private working set;
- native heap/process memory if exposed;
- GPU process memory where available;
- number of renderer processes/webContents.

EVENT LOOP / RESPONSIVENESS

- renderer event-loop lag;
- main-process event-loop lag where practical;
- long-task count/duration;
- project-open latency;
- session-open latency;
- command round-trip latency;
- renderer heartbeat latency.

CARDINALITY COUNTERS

At minimum inspect growth in:

- timers;
- intervals;
- event listeners;
- IPC listeners;
- subscriptions;
- store subscribers;
- React roots/portals where measurable;
- notifications;
- active/fresh marks;
- audit campaigns;
- sessions retained in renderer memory;
- messages retained in renderer memory;
- pending saves;
- pending retries;
- provider probes;
- quota-window timers;
- SAIMAIL pollers;
- worker watches;
- background jobs;
- WebSocket/SSE/stream handles;
- object URLs;
- file watchers;
- child processes.

LOG VOLUME

Track:
- bytes/minute;
- repeated identical messages;
- repeated RPC calls by method;
- write frequency.

T-133 previously found a catastrophic Settings loop that generated millions of
RPC calls.

Explicitly run the same style of RPC/log frequency census again across all
surfaces during the soak.

No surface may continuously hammer an RPC merely because React re-rendered.

A3. BUILD A PERFORMANCE TIMELINE

The final evidence must correlate degradation with growth.

Example shape:

00:15
heap X
rss Y
listeners Z
project-open 120 ms
event-loop p95 8 ms

03:00
...

06:00
...

09:00
...

Do not simply say "memory grew".

Find what grew WITH it.

A4. HEAP RETENTION ANALYSIS

Take renderer heap snapshots at least:

- after warm-up;
- before visible degradation;
- during degradation if reproduced.

Compare retained objects.

Look especially for:

- full message histories retained for inactive projects;
- closed session components;
- stale task streams;
- old tool-call payloads;
- audit artifacts;
- Markdown render trees;
- syntax-highlight trees;
- Monaco/editor state if applicable;
- provider response objects;
- completed worker/session state;
- detached DOM nodes;
- stale closures retaining project/session state;
- unbounded Map/Set/store entries;
- old project projections;
- duplicate subscriptions;
- cached screenshots/blobs/object URLs;
- notification history;
- logs mirrored into renderer state.

Do not assume JavaScript heap is the only cause.

Also inspect native/Electron/GPU growth.

A5. INACTIVE CHAT / SESSION MEMORY BOUNDARY

The operator explicitly notes that completed chats do not need to remain fully
hot in memory forever.

Define a correct two-tier state model:

DURABLE STATE
Stored on disk/service side and recoverable.

HOT RENDERER STATE
Only what is needed for current/recent interaction.

Inactive completed chats/sessions should not keep their complete rendered state
resident indefinitely.

Implement a bounded policy where appropriate:

- current session: fully hot;
- a small recent-session working set: cached;
- older inactive sessions: unloaded from renderer memory;
- reopen: lazily hydrate from durable storage.

Do NOT delete durable conversation history merely to save RAM.

"Not needed in memory" != "delete user data."

Where long message lists remain visible, use appropriate virtualization/windowing
rather than mounting thousands of message/tool nodes.

Do not unload an actively streaming or unsaved session.

A6. BOUNDED CACHES

Audit every cache/history collection for explicit bounds.

Every potentially unbounded cache must have one of:

- LRU/size bound;
- age bound;
- lifecycle invalidation;
- durable backing with hot subset;
- proof that cardinality is naturally bounded.

Pay special attention to project/session caches because the reported failure
appears after long autonomous work.

A7. LISTENER / TIMER LEAK AUDIT — RUNTIME, NOT STRING SEARCH

T-67 used a source census and explicit cleanup-token inspection.

This time also prove runtime counts.

Repeatedly:

mount surface
-> unmount surface
-> mount again

and verify listener/subscription/timer counts return to baseline.

Cover at least:

- project switching;
- session switching;
- Settings;
- SAIHOME;
- scheduler;
- quota displays;
- ProTrail;
- sounds;
- SAIMAIL;
- audits;
- worker status;
- global keyboard shortcuts.

A8. BLANK PROJECT / NOTHING OPENS

Treat the reported empty selected-project surface as a P0 companion symptom.

Trace:

project click
-> selected project state
-> project/session fetch
-> service response
-> renderer store
-> active session resolution
-> render

Determine whether during degraded state:

- service requests never execute;
- requests execute but responses are starved;
- renderer state update is dropped;
- stale memo/cache returns empty;
- selected project points at unloaded state without hydration;
- an exception is swallowed;
- renderer event loop is saturated;
- React render never commits;
- RPC queue is flooded;
- background process is dead;
- resource exhaustion prevents new operations.

Do not fix this with an unconditional UI reload unless the root cause proves that
a recoverable renderer boundary genuinely needs recreation.

Required invariant:

Selecting a valid project must converge to one of:

- loaded project UI;
- explicit loading state;
- explicit recoverable error.

NEVER a permanently blank apparently-working surface.

Add a convergence timeout with diagnostics if needed.

A9. SELF-HEALING, ONLY AFTER ROOT CAUSE

After fixing the real accumulation source, add safe resilience where useful.

Examples allowed:

- unload stale inactive renderer caches;
- restart a dead optional helper;
- reconnect a failed local stream;
- rehydrate a selected session;
- retry an idempotent projection;
- compact finished transient state.

Do NOT use:
- periodic full ZAICODE restart;
- destructive cache wipe;
- arbitrary renderer reload every N hours

as the primary fix.

If a renderer self-recovery mechanism is added, it must preserve:

- active tasks;
- unsaved composer text;
- session identity;
- project selection;
- running workers;
- SAIPEN state.

A10. PERFORMANCE BUDGET

Establish measured budgets from a clean post-warm-up baseline.

At minimum acceptance must prove:

- no monotonic unbounded heap growth under bounded workload;
- no monotonic listener/timer/subscription growth;
- no runaway RPC/log loop;
- event-loop responsiveness remains stable;
- project/session opening remains usable after 9+ hours;
- no 1-2 FPS collapse;
- no permanently blank project surface;
- idle CPU returns near the warm baseline when agents are idle.

For automated soak acceptance, use relative drift from post-warm-up baseline.

Suggested starting gates, refine from evidence:

- post-GC retained renderer heap after the soak should remain within a defensible
  bounded range of post-warm-up under the same bounded working set;
- listener/timer/subscription counts must plateau;
- p95 project-open latency must not degrade by multiples over baseline;
- p95 renderer heartbeat/event-loop lag must not show sustained pathological
  growth.

Do not run forced GC periodically in production.
Diagnostic GC is allowed only in controlled profiling runs.

======================================================================
TRACK B — QUOTA CIRCUIT BREAKER / SAIFREN FAILOVER
======================================================================

B1. ONE AUTHORITATIVE ROUTING DECISION

Inspect the current provider/model execution path.

Do not bolt fallback logic separately into every UI button.

There must be one authoritative effective-route resolver used by:

- composer send;
- Retry;
- auto-continue;
- scheduler;
- prepared tasks;
- subagents/crew where applicable;
- workflow runs;
- background automation.

B2. QUOTA EXHAUSTION CLASSIFICATION

Recognize provider/account exhaustion using structured evidence where available:

- explicit quota telemetry;
- plan limit state;
- authoritative remaining=0;
- resetAt in the future;
- provider-specific quota error;
- HTTP/status error classified as quota exhaustion.

Do not classify ordinary:
- network errors;
- model-not-found;
- auth failure;
- malformed request;
- server 500

as quota exhaustion.

B3. CIRCUIT BREAKER

When a provider/account/plan is proven quota-exhausted:

mark the route unavailable until:

- its known reset time;
- fresh telemetry proves recovery;
- operator explicitly refreshes/re-enables it where appropriate.

While the circuit is open:

ZAICODE must NOT keep hammering the same exhausted route on every task/retry.

The UI should show the route as temporarily exhausted with the known reset.

No noisy repeated provider calls.

B4. SAIFREN FALLBACK

When the selected paid/subscription route is exhausted and SAIFREN is available:

automatically resolve to SAIFREN according to the product fallback policy.

Required behavior:

selected GLM
-> GLM quota exhausted
-> circuit opens
-> effective route becomes SAIFREN
-> task continues without user babysitting.

The transition must be visible/truthful in the UI.

Do not silently claim GLM while executing SAIFREN.

Display something equivalent to:

GLM unavailable until <time>
Using SAIFREN fallback

Keep the user's preferred model selection separate from the temporary effective
route so it can return after quota recovery if policy allows.

B5. ANTI-FLAP

Do not alternate:

GLM -> SAIFREN -> GLM -> SAIFREN

on every poll.

Use the known reset/health state and hysteresis.

When reset occurs, probe/revalidate before routing production work back.

B6. RETRY MUST USE CURRENT SELECTION

This is a separate confirmed bug.

Current reported behavior:

1. GLM attempt fails due to limit.
2. Operator changes selection to SAIFREN.
3. Operator clicks Retry.
4. ZAICODE still retries GLM.

Root cause likely involves a retry object retaining the failed attempt's route.

Trace it.

Define the contract:

RETRY CONTENT
May reuse:
- prompt/task payload;
- attachments;
- relevant run context.

RETRY ROUTING
Must recompute from CURRENT execution selection/policy unless the action is
explicitly named "retry same route".

A normal Retry must not blindly preserve a stale failed provider/model binding.

Tests:

GLM fails
-> select SAIFREN
-> Retry
-> only SAIFREN dispatch occurs.

Also:

provider A fails
-> no manual selection change
-> circuit breaker chooses SAIFREN
-> Retry does not hit A again.

And:

failure unrelated to quota
-> route policy behaves according to normal retry rules.

B7. EXACTLY-ONCE

Fallback must not create duplicate logical turns.

One logical task may have multiple transport attempts, but:
- one visible user request;
- one authoritative active attempt at a time;
- late result from abandoned provider must not overwrite the accepted fallback
  result.

Use stable attempt/run identities.

======================================================================
TRACK C — ANTIGRAVITY / ZCODE ROLLING WINDOW TRUTH
======================================================================

C1. REOPEN THE T-143 ASSUMPTION

T-143 previously implemented:

- ZaicodeLimitWindow.rollingFrom;
- synthetic rolling anchors;
- Antigravity/ZCode starters;
- parser retention at exactly 0%;
- continuous countdown semantics.

The current operator report proves live behavior is still incorrect.

Do not close this as "already fixed by T-143."

Treat it as a regression or incomplete live implementation.

C2. "STARTS AT FIRST USE" MUST NOT REMAIN FOR SUPPORTED AUTO-ROLLED PLANS

For Antigravity/ZCode plans that ZAICODE is explicitly capable of maintaining:

the product must not sit indefinitely displaying:

starts at first use

while waiting for the user.

At 0% consumption, ZAICODE should initiate the minimal safe window-start action
once per window where the provider contract allows it.

Then it must maintain a durable authoritative anchor.

Do not repeatedly send paid/wasteful model traffic every sweep.

A start action must be:
- minimal;
- idempotent per window;
- rate-limited;
- attributable;
- safe to retry.

If a vendor cannot actually be started automatically, do not fake a running
countdown. Mark that route truthfully as unsupported for auto-start.

C3. WINDOW STATE MUST SURVIVE SWEEPS AND RESTART

If ZAICODE knows:

window started at T0

then repeated quota refreshes that omit a fraction must NOT reset T0 to "now".

Persist enough state to keep the same real window identity through:
- polling;
- renderer restart;
- ZAICODE restart.

But never carry a stale anchor past a proven reset.

C4. "WINDOW STARTED 13 MIN AGO" BUT NOT VISIBLE

Trace every window presentation surface.

One authoritative quota-window state must drive:

- provider tile;
- model/subscription selector;
- reset chip;
- tooltip;
- SAIHOME if shown there;
- scheduler/off-peak availability;
- fallback/circuit-breaker policy.

If backend state says a window started 13 minutes ago, a user-facing active
window/countdown must not disappear from the surfaces that claim to show that
plan.

No separate competing interpretations of:
- startsOnUse;
- rollingFrom;
- resetsAt;
- remainingPercent.

C5. REAL LIVE ACCEPTANCE

Parser/unit tests are not enough this time.

Where credentials are available and safe:

perform read-only/live quota telemetry and the minimum intended starter action
against actual Antigravity and ZCode accounts.

Record:

before:
consumption
window status
reset

start action:
exact classification, without leaking credentials

after:
window anchor
resetAt
UI presentation

wait through multiple sweeps and prove the anchor remains stable.

If live credentials are unavailable:
mark only this final vendor observation as OPERATOR_REQUIRED.

Do not claim the live regression fixed solely from synthetic JSON.

======================================================================
CROSS-TRACK OBSERVABILITY
======================================================================

Add a compact diagnostic snapshot suitable for future bug reports.

It should be possible to export current ZAICODE runtime health including:

- application uptime;
- process memory;
- renderer heap;
- event-loop health;
- project/session hot-cache counts;
- active workers;
